"The words humazah and lumazah are intensive forms derived from the roots \"hamz\" and \"lamz\". \"Hamz\" means to pinch with the hand, prod, or break; while \"lamz\" means to reproach or disparage. These two words denote people who backbite, slander, mock, and gossip about others. According to some commentators, humazah refers to an assault committed with the hand, and lumazah to one committed with the tongue; while according to others, humazah denotes what is done to a person's face, and lumazah what is done behind their back. In general, these attributes are used for those who have made it a habit to wound people's honor and dignity, find fault, and mock.\n\nOne of the primary characteristics of those who adopt this habit is their fondness for amassing wealth and constantly counting it. They think that their wealth will make them immortal. Yet true salvation lies in faith and righteous deeds. By Allah, these arrogant and mocking individuals will surely be cast into the Hell called \"Hutamah\".\n\nHutamah is a specially kindled fire symbolizing Allah's wrath. It is unlike ordinary fires; it does not merely burn physical bodies, but penetrates right into souls and hearts. The closing of that fire over the people of Hell is depicted as its doors being shut tight with long pillars and props. This emphasizes the permanence and severity of the torment.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, after the disobedient believers are taken out of the Fire, the doors of Hell will be closed upon the disbelievers with covers, nails, and pillars of fire, leaving neither a breath of air nor an escape route. Allah will abandon them as if having forgotten them; the people of Paradise will be amidst bounties, while the people of Hell will be unable to ask for any help, and only their groans will be heard. O Allah, protect us from the fire of Hell and admit us into Paradise along with the righteous!"